  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potential for heavy rain and flooding Sunday-\nTuesday\n\nStrengthening south-southwest flow in advance of a slow moving\nupper trough in the Ohio Valley will drive anomalous 1.5-2\" PW \ninto CPA along and ahead of an approaching cold front on Sunday.\nStrong warm advection and moisture transport/LLJ combined with \nfavorable upper dynamics should support active/elevated \nconvection reaching west-central PA as early as late Saturday \nnight with max POPs centered on the 24hr Sunday window. Cloud \ncover and early day rain showers continue to cast a shadow on \nconditional severe wx potential through Sunday afternoon and \nevening; however stronger shear values amidst a relatively low \nCAPE environment is worth calling out particularly if there are\nbreaks of sunshine. \n\nIt now appears there will be at least some potential for heavy \nrain/flooding through early next week as the upper trough \ncloses off or trends slower to eject out of the Ohio Valley. \nWhile WPC trimmed the level 2/4 excessive rain outlook back a \nbit over NW PA for Sunday/D3, we will need to watch for sneaky \n+RA signal to shift over eastern portions of the CWA where the \nlevel 1 marginal risk will be expanded with the next ERO \nissuance later this afternoon. \n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\nJuly = record-setting month in terms of convective warnings. \nWFO CTP issued 12 Tornado Warnings in July, which is the most \nsince 1996 when we issued 25. 95 Severe Thunderstorm Warnings is\nthe 3rd most in any July on record, behind 1996 (96) and 2021 \n(122). Year-to- date, 226 Severe Thunderstorm Warnings is the \nmost on record, passing 2019 (221), and 24 Tornado Warnings is \n4th most behind 1998 (55), 2011 (37), and 1996 (30). 250 total \nSevere Thunderstorm Warnings + Tornado Warnings is the most on \nrecord year-to-date, passing 2019 (241).",
